  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Yidu holds the FY26 cost reset (admin and R&D cut ~RMB100m) and grows ~10% a year as pharma real-world-evidence and clinical-trial work (17 of the top 20 MNCs as clients) and the Hui Min Bao city-insurance/Care business compound, while the hospital/government big-data segment, budget-constrained by local-government finances, grows slowly. Receivable impairments normalise. Pre-SBC EBITDA margin reaches 15% (owner-FCF ~9-10%) by year 8. The RMB3.35bn treasury is preserved and not wasted.",
    "bull": "YiduCore/medical large-model deployment converts into recurring platform revenue across hospitals, pharma and insurers; China's push on medical AI and data-element policy accelerates hospital procurement; Life Sciences wins more global RWE mandates. Revenue compounds mid-teens for five years and the software-like mix lifts pre-SBC EBITDA margin to 22%.",
    "bear": "FY26's breakeven was a one-off cost cut, not operating leverage: local-government and hospital IT budgets stay squeezed, AI commoditises the data-platform offer (large-model vendors and hospital in-house builds), pharma RWE spend in China stays soft, and receivables keep being impaired. Revenue grows ~2-3%, EBITDA hovers near zero and the business burns cash for most of the decade; terminal value close to nil. Value collapses to the net cash less the burn."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Yidu's value is mostly its balance sheet: ~RMB3.3/share of net financial assets (term deposits, IG bank notes, cash) against a quote that converts to ~RMB2.45/share. The operating business is valued on owner FCF built from a near-breakeven core (FY26 profit was treasury interest, not operations). Even the bear case, where the operation is worth little, leaves value near net cash less the burn; the question is whether management and the controlling holder let shareholders keep the cash.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Cash access / governance: offshore-listed VIE structure; the market already prices the treasury at ~0.73x. Buybacks (RMB260m in FY26) are the strongest counter-evidence.",
      "FY26 operating breakeven may reflect a one-off staff-cost cut and lower impairments rather than durable leverage.",
      "Customer budget risk: hospitals, local governments and Hui Min Bao programmes depend on public finances.",
      "Receivables (RMB378m + RMB65m contract assets on RMB819m revenue) and their impairment.",
      "Price has fallen ~53% since March 2026 with no H1 FY27 filing yet extracted; the interim (due ~Nov 2026) could reset cash or margins.",
      "Unfunded VC fund commitment (~US$26.8m) and strategic investments may divert cash."
    ]
  },
